Guest-compatible libgomp builds
==============================

This is a contributor tool for developing and testing Phantom Tracker's
libgomp changes before they are available in upstream GCC packages. It
provides a Debian 12 amd64 build environment matching the guests currently
provisioned by scripts/create_vm.sh. Compilation runs on the host in a
container; GCC does not need to be built or installed in the guests.

The container is independent of VM creation, the pvsched driver, and eBPF
builds. It is not required by users installing packaged components. Once
compatible GCC packages provide the changes, this tool can be retired.

Only the build environment and these instructions live in this repository.
Keep GCC sources, patches, build output, and published runtimes outside it.


Build on the host
-----------------

Requires Docker on an x86_64 Linux host and permission to access its daemon.
Use sudo docker instead of docker if your account cannot access the socket.
Run from the Phantom Tracker root:

    docker build --platform linux/amd64 \
        -t phantom-libgomp:debian12 dev-tools/libgomp

Set paths to an existing GCC source tree containing the desired changes
and a new, empty build directory outside the repository:

    GCC_SOURCE="$HOME/src/gcc"
    GCC_BUILD="$HOME/src/build/gcc-baseline"
    mkdir -p "$GCC_BUILD"

Use a fresh build directory for each source revision or configuration.
Do not reuse objects built against the host's glibc. For a Git checkout,
record the source revision and tracked changes before building:

    test "$(git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" branch --show-current)" = baseline
    test "$(git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" rev-parse HEAD)" = "$(git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" rev-parse baseline-v0)"
    test -z "$(git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" status --porcelain)"
    GCC_REVISION=$(git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" rev-parse HEAD)
    SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=$(git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" show -s --format=%ct HEAD)
    BUILD_IMAGE=$(docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' phantom-libgomp:debian12)
    printf '%s\n' "$GCC_REVISION" > "$GCC_BUILD/source-revision.txt"
    printf '%s\n' "$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH" > "$GCC_BUILD/source-date-epoch.txt"
    git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" diff --binary HEAD > "$GCC_BUILD/source-changes.patch"
    git -C "$GCC_SOURCE" status --short > "$GCC_BUILD/source-status.txt"
    printf '%s\n' "$BUILD_IMAGE" > "$GCC_BUILD/container-image.txt"

Run the checks above in a shell with set -e, or stop if any check fails.
This baseline uses the clean baseline branch at baseline-v0; select a new
reference and build directory for modified sources. Keep the source tree
unchanged throughout the build. The patch records tracked changes only.

Build with the source mounted read-only and output owned by your host user:

    docker run --rm --platform linux/amd64 \
        --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" \
        --mount "type=bind,src=$GCC_SOURCE,dst=/src,readonly" \
        --mount "type=bind,src=$GCC_BUILD,dst=/build" \
        --env SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH="$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH" \
        --env LC_ALL=C --env TZ=UTC --env BUILD_JOBS="$(nproc)" \
        "$BUILD_IMAGE" bash -c '
            set -euo pipefail
            dpkg-query -W > build-packages.txt
            /src/configure \
                --build=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu \
                --host=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu \
                --target=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu \
                --disable-bootstrap \
                --disable-multilib \
                --disable-nls \
                --disable-libcc1 \
                --enable-languages=c \
                --with-arch=x86-64 --with-tune=generic \
                CFLAGS="-O2 -g -ffile-prefix-map=/src=/usr/src/gcc -ffile-prefix-map=/build=/usr/src/gcc-build" \
                CXXFLAGS="-O2 -g -ffile-prefix-map=/src=/usr/src/gcc -ffile-prefix-map=/build=/usr/src/gcc-build" \
                CFLAGS_FOR_TARGET="-O2 -g -ffile-prefix-map=/src=/usr/src/gcc -ffile-prefix-map=/build=/usr/src/gcc-build" \
                2>&1 | tee configure.log
            make -j"$BUILD_JOBS" all-target-libgomp 2>&1 | tee build.log
            lib=x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/libgomp/.libs/libgomp.so.1
            test -r "$lib"
            readelf -d "$lib" > runtime-dynamic.txt
            readelf --version-info "$lib" > runtime-versions.txt
            readelf -n "$lib" > runtime-notes.txt
            ldd "$lib" > runtime-dependencies.txt
            sha256sum "$(readlink -f "$lib")" > runtime-sha256.txt
        '

The build stops when all-target-libgomp succeeds. This target builds the C
compiler and libgcc prerequisites, then libgomp; it does not build the other
GCC runtimes or install GCC. Do not interrupt a general make when a library
file first appears: publication requires successful target completion.

Only the C front end is enabled to reduce build time. libgomp's Fortran ABI
wrappers (fortran.c) are included regardless; applications can still use the
guest's native gfortran. Compiler-specific omp_lib/OpenACC Fortran modules
from the custom GCC are outside this runtime-only workflow.

BUILD_JOBS uses the number of processing units available to the host shell,
as reported by nproc. The runtime is produced at:

    <GCC_BUILD>/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/libgomp/.libs/libgomp.so.1

Inspect runtime-dependencies.txt for missing libraries, runtime-versions.txt
for symbol requirements, and runtime-notes.txt for expected USDT probes in
patched builds. A baseline without those patches need not have the probes.

Use the same source revision and configure options for baseline and modified
builds, except for the changes under comparison. Avoid -march=native when
publishing a runtime for guests with different exposed CPU features.

GCC build prerequisites and configuration options:
https://gcc.gnu.org/install/prerequisites.html
https://gcc.gnu.org/install/configure.html


Publish and use in guests
-------------------------

Publish only completed runtime files to a new host directory, preserving
relative symlinks. For example:

    LIBGOMP_RELEASE="$PWD/hg_shared_dir/libgomp/baseline-v0"
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$LIBGOMP_RELEASE")"
    mkdir "$LIBGOMP_RELEASE"
    cp -a "$GCC_BUILD"/x86_64-pc-linux-gnu/libgomp/.libs/libgomp.so* \
        "$LIBGOMP_RELEASE/"
    cp "$GCC_BUILD"/source-* "$GCC_BUILD/container-image.txt" \
        "$GCC_BUILD/build-packages.txt" "$GCC_BUILD/configure.log" \
        "$GCC_BUILD"/runtime-*.txt \
        "$LIBGOMP_RELEASE/"

Preserve this directory unchanged while guests use it. Publish each new
build to a different directory. Several identical Debian 12 amd64 guests
can use the same completed release; each VM has its own qcow2 disk.

Start each VM from the project root with --mode=dev --shared-dir=./hg_shared_dir
(or ../hg_shared_dir when running from scripts/). Sharing is read-only by
default. Follow scripts/README to mount it at /mnt/hostshare.
The container does not need to remain running. Its published host directory
is what QEMU exports.

Use 9p only to transfer the completed release. Inside each guest, copy it
onto the guest's own disk before linking, running applications, or attaching
USDT probes:

    GUEST_LIBGOMP="$HOME/libgomp-releases/baseline-v0"
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$GUEST_LIBGOMP")"
    mkdir -- "$GUEST_LIBGOMP" && \
        cp -a --no-preserve=ownership /mnt/hostshare/libgomp/baseline-v0/. "$GUEST_LIBGOMP/"

The copy refuses to reuse an existing release directory. Keep each local
release unchanged during experiments and use a new directory for another build. Verify that the
library is on the guest disk rather than the 9p mount:

    findmnt -T "$GUEST_LIBGOMP/libgomp.so.1" -o TARGET,FSTYPE,OPTIONS

Compile applications and store results on the guest's local disk. To link
an application with the local runtime, add these flags after its object
files, while retaining its usual -fopenmp compilation/link options:

    -L"$GUEST_LIBGOMP" -Wl,-rpath,"$GUEST_LIBGOMP" -l:libgomp.so.1

The explicit library name selects the versioned shared library. Verify the
result inside the guest:

    ldd /path/to/application | grep libgomp

It should resolve libgomp.so.1 to the local GUEST_LIBGOMP directory.
Configure the eBPF loaders to attach to that same local file. On Nchang's
branch, their build-time setting is:

    make LIBGOMP_PATH="$GUEST_LIBGOMP/libgomp.so.1"

System libgomp discovery via ldconfig is not sufficient when the application
loads this custom runtime. USDT probe discovery and attachment take place
only on the local copy; they are never attempted on the 9p volume. Verify
the expected probes in the local library before starting the loaders:

    readelf -n "$GUEST_LIBGOMP/libgomp.so.1"

The required probes must be provided by the selected patched GCC sources.
The build image includes systemtap-sdt-dev for sources using sys/sdt.h;
check any source-specific configure options needed to enable those probes.
This release contains only libgomp; other application runtime dependencies
must be supplied by the guest or built for the same userspace.


Reproducing a build
-------------------

Debian 12 provides the guest-compatible userspace, but the debian:12-slim
tag and its apt packages can change. For repeatable experiments, retain
the exact built container image (for example with docker image save), the
source revision and patch, SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH, configure.log, build.log, and
build-packages.txt. The build uses the immutable BUILD_IMAGE ID, fixed
container paths and CPU target, UTC/C locale, and source commit timestamp.
Preserve config.status as the exact configure invocation.

These controls make inputs repeatable; byte-for-byte reproducibility still
needs verification by a second clean build with the same inputs and a
comparison of the published library SHA-256 hashes.

BASE_IMAGE accepts a digest-pinned Debian 12 image via docker build's
--build-arg option. A pinned base alone does not freeze packages fetched
from apt; reusing the saved build image also preserves the installed tools.
Building for a different guest userspace requires a corresponding build
environment and compatibility checks in that guest.
